I love deadlines I like the whooshing  sound they make as they rush past so Big  Finish review this one's actually a  patreon commissioned one because while I  have been doing Big Finish reviews  regularly I'm you know more or less sort  of jumping around occasionally I will  have a vote from the various people on  patreon to various people there my  patronize errs yeah I know them by name  I stick them in the credits at the end  that's one of the benefits to a plug  anyways yeah so normally I just give a  list of Big Finish things that I have  already gotten my hands on either cuz  they were on sale or they were gifted to  me and I let them choose now I had a  patreon Iser who had a very specific  story he wanted me to check out and he  actually commissioned a review and so I  have now listened to Doctor Who unbound  deadlines and this is so this is so  weird it's it's written by Robert  Sherman who has written my favorite Big  Finish stuff so far  quite easily in fact he wrote Jubilee he  wrote um a Holy Terror which is still my  favorite hero chimes of midnight like  he's really really good at writing  Doctor Who and writing Doctor Who for  audio now Doctor Who unbound is a bit of  a weird beast so it's essentially a  series of hour-long what-if stories  completely removed from Doctor Who  continuity and in fact with actors who  have never played the doctor before  taking on whatever iteration of the  doctor the various writers came up with  now what Robert Sherman decided to  tackle was what if Doctor Who never  actually got produced as a TV series  like it was  conceived it was worked on never  actually went to air  that's his what if so this story largely  centers around a aging writer man named  Martin who is in a nursing home and he  is voiced by the incomparable derek  jacobi who I've already had the joy of  listening to as the war master and the  war master series one  I think series two is out now I got to  get my hands on he's so friggin good but  so he's playing this television writer  who was commissioned by Sydney Newman  and Verity Lambert although he mostly  work with Sydney Newman and this is all  fictionalized to write the scripts for  what was going to be Doctor Who but the  show never moved forward early bits were  scripted he was revising he was drafting  I never moved forward he got moved to  other work at the BBC he wrote a bunch  of episodes for a cop drama like a 60s  cop drama that he thinks incredibly  little of but has a pretty sizable  fanbase and it's by far the thing he's  best known for despite the fact of being  an award-winning winning playwright  before starting to write for the BBC so  what this story focuses on is an ageing  writer who believes himself better than  the work he is known for doesn't have a  lot of regard for the people who liked  the show that he is best known for  writing for has a very broken history  with his family is suffering from some  degree of dementia and failing mental  faculties and who can't get the ideas of  Doctor Who completely out of his head  and to try and explain how all this  plays out is incredibly difficult but  not  because it's hard to follow because  Robert Sherman really knows this medium  he knows how to transition stuff  seamlessly from one situation into  another transitioning with the same  voice actors now actually doing  something different just through the  audio medium and what I mean by that is  Derek Jacoby's character he will you  know he'll be talking with this head  nurse at the maybe not the head nurse  but the one who works with the most  often at this nursing home he's talking  about his writing and she leaves and  he's starting to think about revisions  and you know how he can alter and how to  make the story better and suddenly he  himself is the doctor talking to Susan  not about writing the story but being  the doctor and having adventures with  her and names keep popping up that the  the nurse who is Barbara right Ian  Chesterton was was a man that she that  she was engaged to at one point these  names keep popping up we meet Martin's  own son Philip who hates him and the ins  and outs of what we learn about why that  is both in terms of what Martin did that  made his son hate him but also the ways  in which Martin is projecting other  things about his life and faulting his  father for how things have turned out  for him  and that's incredibly well vows because  that could easily have gone either way  that could that could have read either  as he was that he was just too harsh on  this guy or that Martin was unredeemable  II a jerk or it could have read as this  adult son just refusing to move on and  blame me as dad for everything but it  balances everything really well and if  everybody here is so human and it is  it's heartbreaking at times for a  variety of reasons so first off  peek behind the curtain I mentioned this  before because it's come up  intermittently when I've talked about  reviews that touch on these kinds of  things  my grandfather had dementia towards the  end of his life  so things that are able to respectfully  but also realistically and properly  touch on the that kind of thing that  degradation of a mind and the  frustration of both being the person  whose mind is degrading and being  someone trying to interact with someone  who's who has that going on when that's  captured well it's I end up finding a  very stirring of a lot of stuff and this  did that which to me says it did it well  and in terms of the broken history and  the broken relationships that Martin has  with his son and how he is desperate to  try and have a relationship with his  grandson he actually has yet to even  meet and he often doesn't remember he  has he there's a line that he has  basically telling Phillip I screwed that  up it's too late let me meet my grandson  I have a ruin to that yet oh like  there's so much in that there is so much  blind foolish hope in that there is so  much unresolved guilt in that there's  there so much but even while it's  tackling these things about family and  dementia and damage done from parent to  child it's also tackling  the nature of the artist and their  thoughts about their own art how they  feel about what is or isn't liked about  what they did what they wish they were  remembered for the sacrifices they made  for their art and this does something  that I really really appreciated because  so often when you see or hear or read a  story about an artist basically  sacrificing or torpedoing their personal  or home or family life for the sake of  their art even in stories where that's  not portrayed is a good thing  there's still usually an amazing piece  of art that results from that what  happens to Martin is he abandons his you  know this is backstory he abandoned his  wife and his child in the belief that he  was sacrificing them to make great art  and he just never did he worked but he  never made that great art so his  sacrifice was for nothing but he was so  arrogant and so self certain that he was  going to be amazing that he thought so  little of being around and supporting  and offering his energies to people who  loved him and needed him because he  never doubted his own genius and the  moments of jacobi having these moments  of Revelation of looking back and  realizing who he is the kind of man that  he is and has been and continues to be  their often brief because the guy's mind  is so fractured he doesn't hold on to  these thoughts for very long but when  they hit you can even hear it in the  boat and the voice and the vocal  performance it's  it is tragic in a low-key way this this  is this is familial and artistic  melancholy made manifest and for all the  things I'm talking about this is all  done in an hour in one hour you have an  exploration of the arrogance of artists  the degradation of a mind the shattering  of a family the retreat into art as an  escape from whatever doesn't work the  clinging t